1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:04,365
On a shared server, nobody should get access one person at a time.

2
00:00:04,385 --> 00:00:07,868
You put people into groups, and give the group the access.

3
00:00:07,888 --> 00:00:11,431
Add a person, remove a person, and the rules stay the same.

4
00:00:11,901 --> 00:00:16,489
In this video: create, delete, and modify local groups and group memberships.

5
00:00:21,009 --> 00:00:23,592
Here's the task, the way an exam would state it.

6
00:00:24,062 --> 00:00:27,690
Create, delete, and modify local groups and group memberships.

7
00:00:28,160 --> 00:00:32,242
We'll create groups, add users to a group, rename a group, remove a member,

8
00:00:32,262 --> 00:00:33,281
and delete a group.

9
00:00:39,801 --> 00:00:45,262
Every local group is one line in the file /etc/group. You see it with getent group.

10
00:00:45,282 --> 00:00:47,837
It has four parts, separated by colons:

11
00:00:47,857 --> 00:00:52,988
the group name, a password placeholder, the group ID, and the list of members,

12
00:00:53,008 --> 00:00:54,309
separated by commas.

13
00:01:04,779 --> 00:01:10,081
groupadd creates a group. groupmod changes it. groupdel deletes it.

14
00:01:10,101 --> 00:01:13,179
And usermod -aG adds a user to a group.

15
00:01:13,199 --> 00:01:16,834
In this demo the lab changes nothing silently.

16
00:01:25,354 --> 00:01:30,791
Create a group: sudo groupadd devs. getent group devs shows its line:

17
00:01:30,811 --> 00:01:35,061
the name, an x, the group ID 1001, and no members yet.

18
00:01:35,081 --> 00:01:40,993
With -g, you choose the group ID yourself. Here, the group audit gets 3000.

19
00:01:52,513 --> 00:01:57,747
Now two users, alice and bob. We create them first, as in the last video.

20
00:01:57,767 --> 00:02:02,642
To add a user to a group: usermod -aG, then the group, and the user.

21
00:02:02,662 --> 00:02:06,097
-G is the list of extra groups. -a means append:

22
00:02:06,117 --> 00:02:11,639
keep the groups the user already has. Without -a, usermod would replace them.

23
00:02:11,659 --> 00:02:16,246
We run it for each user. The group line now lists alice and bob.

24
00:02:16,266 --> 00:02:18,406
And id alice shows her groups:

25
00:02:18,426 --> 00:02:22,652
her own private group, which every new user gets, and devs.

26
00:02:38,172 --> 00:02:43,545
To change a group, use groupmod. -n sets a new name. Devs becomes developers.

27
00:02:43,565 --> 00:02:46,557
The group ID and the members stay the same.

28
00:02:55,077 --> 00:03:00,593
To remove one member, use gpasswd, -d, the user, and the group. It tells us:

29
00:03:00,613 --> 00:03:05,254
removing user bob from group developers. Now only alice is left.

30
00:03:13,774 --> 00:03:18,151
To delete a group: groupdel. We create a group called temp, then delete it.

31
00:03:18,171 --> 00:03:20,495
getent finds nothing: the group is gone.

32
00:03:29,015 --> 00:03:32,451
Our five steps: task, demo, check, reboot, and your turn.

33
00:03:33,971 --> 00:03:36,909
We reboot. The group developers still has alice.

34
00:03:36,929 --> 00:03:40,607
Group changes are saved in a file, so they survive a reboot.

35
00:03:48,127 --> 00:03:54,267
Your turn. The lab silently creates the users erin and frank, and a group called temp, first.

36
00:03:54,287 --> 00:03:58,705
Create the group ops with group ID 4000. Add erin and frank to ops.

37
00:03:58,725 --> 00:04:03,275
Delete the group temp. Run the checker, then reboot and run it again.

38
00:04:12,745 --> 00:04:14,496
Try before you open the solution.

39
00:04:20,016 --> 00:04:22,407
Next up: configure privileged access.

40
00:04:22,877 --> 00:04:25,779
Subscribe to follow the series, and let's keep going.
